Lab 13: Scale Ansible Platform with High Availability
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Design an Ansible Automation Controller High Availability (HA) architecture using multiple controller nodes, a shared PostgreSQL database, and a load balancer
Write and execute Ansible playbooks that configure PostgreSQL, HAProxy, and controller node clustering settings
Simulate node failure scenarios and validate that automation services remain available through a load balancer
Build a Python monitoring script that measures HA endpoint availability and response time over a defined test window
Interpret uptime and response-time reports to identify weak points in an HA deployment
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Ansible concepts (inventories, playbooks, modules)
Familiarity with Linux system administration (services, packages, file permissions)
Basic knowledge of PostgreSQL and load balancing concepts (HAProxy)
A practice environment with at least 3 Linux VMs/containers you control (this lab uses generic hostnames/IPs that you should replace with your own lab addresses)
Required tools
Install the following on your control node (the machine from which you run ansible and ansible-playbook):

# Install Ansible and the Python HTTP library used by the monitoring script
python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip
python3 -m pip install ansible requests

# Verify versions
ansible --version
python3 -c "import requests; print(requests.__version__)"
This lab treats the Automation Controller HA install itself as out of scope (it requires subscription-gated installer bundles). Instead, we focus on the parts every learner can run and verify in a self-owned lab: the supporting PostgreSQL configuration, the HAProxy load balancer that would front real controller nodes, failover simulation against that load balancer, and a Python-based HA monitoring tool. This keeps every command in this lab real, runnable, and checkable without depending on proprietary installer media.

